NotesString/You can open this notes window for any project by selecting View->Notes...

This example shows activity on the bus of an Intel 8051 series microcontroller.

The 8051 multiplexes its data and the lower 8 bits of its address on pins AD[7..0].
The signals in group "AD[7..0]" are sampled from these pins on the 8051.

The circuit under test uses an external TTL latch to capture the lower 8 bits of the 
address when ALE from the controller is high. The signals in group "Latched A[7..0]" 
are sampled from the outputs of this latch. 

When the 8051 sets PSEN high, it is accessing data memory. When PSEN is low,
the 8051 is accessing program memory. 

With this information, we can observe the following memory cycles:

   At cursor A the 8051 writes the value 0h to data memory address 2D1h.

   At cursor B the 8051 reads the value D2h from program memory address 2CDh.

You can see these memory cycles in the state list tab as well. Note that you can quickly
scroll to cursor A or B by pressing the <A> or <B> keys on the keyboard.

This example shows one of the simplest possible trigger schemes. Trigger occurs on the 
falling edge of the 8051's WR signal.

Note that you can specify group data formats and optionally select a specific color for any 
waveform by right-clicking in the left column of the waveform table. In this example the
colors for XTAL1 and Latched A[7..0] have been changed to highlight these waveforms.

Notice that sample compression allowed the LogicPort to capture 19.3K samples, 
more than nine times its buffer size. To see the effect (on compression) of adding a 
constantly toggling clock to the waveform table, open the file "8051 With XTAL1.LPF".

Please note that all of the example files are "read only" so that they remain intact
for future reference. You can change settings in these files for learning purposes, 
but the changes cannot be saved. You can however use the "Save As" option to 
save an editable copy of any example file.
/
